## Claim
Text: "A law called The Flora and Fauna Act classified aboriginal people as animals until Australian voters overturned it in the 1960s."
Claim author: Lost In History
Claim date: October 11, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20201022032602/https://www.facebook.com/lostinhistorypics/posts/2021427674656146

## Initial Q&A
### Was there an official law in Australia, at either the federal or state/territory level, titled "The Flora and Fauna Act"?
Answer: While there is no law titled exactly "The Flora and Fauna Act," there is a real Victorian law called the **Flora and Fauna Guarantee Act 1988**, which "provides the main legal framework for the protection of Victoria's biodiversity, native plants and animals and ecological communities." This law deals exclusively with conservation of native species and ecological communities—it has no connection to the classification of Aboriginal people. There is no evidence of any Australian federal or state/territory law titled "The Flora and Fauna Act" that classified Aboriginal people as animals; this is a persistent myth that has been widely debunked.

Source URL: https://www.gbcma.vic.gov.au/our-region/land_and_biodiversity/relevant_policy_legislation/flora_and_fauna_guarantee_act_and_regulations

### What was the full legal title, jurisdiction, and year of enactment for the legislation referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act"?
Answer: The legislation commonly referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act" has no full legal title, jurisdiction, or year of enactment because it is an urban myth; as the source states, "The Flora and Fauna Act does not exist now, nor has it ever existed. It is a very powerful and successful example of the urban myth."

Source URL: https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/shareena-clanton-is-like-many-of-us-a-victim-of-the-flora-and-fauna-act-myth/m40dgtgah

### Did the legislation referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act" contain explicit text that defined or classified Aboriginal Australians as animals, flora, or fauna?
Answer: No. The search result, based on extensive research, indicates that no such legislation existed. The blog post's author could not find any "Flora and Fauna Act" in any Australian jurisdiction, and scholars who investigated the claim concluded it is a myth. Notably, Professor Marcia Langton confirmed that "The story … is a myth that has circulated in the Aboriginal movement since the 1970s. It started as a joke and I think it was Lester or Jerry Bostock who first described our status with this phrase at a FCAATSI conference in Canberra in 1977. It was an attempt to say that at that time we were regarded as less than animals." While various state laws and government departments (such as Western Australia's "Aborigines and Fisheries" department) administered Aboriginal affairs in ways that were deeply discriminatory, there is no evidence of any legislation containing explicit text classifying Aboriginal Australians as animals, flora, or fauna.

Source URL: https://clubtroppo.com.au/2013/06/03/were-indigenous-people-regulated-by-the-flora-fauna-act/

### If the legislation did not contain explicit text, was it interpreted or administered by government authorities in a way that effectively classified Aboriginal Australians as animals, flora, or fauna?
Answer: The search result indicates that under the Flora and Fauna Act, Aboriginal Australians were explicitly "classified them as animals, not human beings" by government authorities until the 1960s.

Source URL: https://www.reddit.com/r/pics/comments/3wf4hi/australia_until_60s_aborigines_came_under_the/

### What was the primary stated purpose of the legislation referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act" (e.g., for environmental conservation, land management)?
Answer: The legislation referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act" never existed and therefore has no primary stated purpose; it is a myth, as confirmed by research: "The Flora and Fauna Act does not exist now, nor has it ever existed. It is a very powerful and successful example of the urban myth."

Source URL: https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/shareena-clanton-is-like-many-of-us-a-victim-of-the-flora-and-fauna-act-myth/m40dgtgah

### Is there historical evidence, such as official government documents or parliamentary debates, showing that Aboriginal Australians were subject to regulations intended for animals or plants under this legislation?
Answer: There is no historical evidence in official government documents or parliamentary debates showing that Aboriginal Australians were subject to regulations intended for animals or plants under a "Flora and Fauna Act," as experts and comprehensive searches have confirmed no such legislation ever existed. The claim is a pervasive myth, with its origins traced to misunderstandings of government department names and the symbolic narrative surrounding the 1967 referendum.

Source URL: https://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-03-20/fact-check-flora-and-fauna-1967-referendum/9550650

### In what year was the legislation referenced as "The Flora and Fauna Act" formally repealed or overturned?
Answer: The legislation commonly referred to as the "Flora and Fauna Act" never existed; therefore, there is no year in which it was formally repealed or overturned. The source states this is a myth, and a fact check found "Aboriginal people in Australia have never been covered by a flora and fauna act, either under federal or state law."

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flora_and_Fauna_Act_myth

### Was the repeal or overturning of the legislation conducted via a direct vote by Australian citizens (a national or state referendum)?
Answer: Yes, the 1967 referendum was a direct national vote by Australian citizens, with over 90 per cent voting "yes" to change the constitution regarding laws for Aboriginal people.

Source URL: https://www.abc.net.au/news/2017-05-26/larissa-behrendt-mythbusting-the-1967-referendum/8349858

### If not by referendum, what was the legislative or governmental process (e.g., an act of Parliament) that led to the legislation being overturned, and what prompted that process in the 1960s?
Answer: There was no legislative process to overturn a "Flora and Fauna Act" because such an act did not exist; the claim is a myth. The 1967 referendum altered the Constitution to allow the federal government to make laws for Aboriginal people and to include them in population counts, but it did not overturn any fauna-related legislation.

Source URL: https://clubtroppo.com.au/2013/06/03/were-indigenous-people-regulated-by-the-flora-fauna-act/

### Do mainstream Australian historians and historical archives acknowledge the existence and impact of this specific "Flora and Fauna Act" as described in the claim?
Answer: No, mainstream Australian historians and historical archives do not acknowledge the existence of the "Flora and Fauna Act" as described in the claim. Research by scholars such as Samuel Byrnand, a political scientist specializing in Australian legislative history, concludes that "The Flora and Fauna Act does not exist now, nor has it ever existed. It is a very powerful and successful example of the urban myth," noting that while Aboriginal people faced profound legislative racism and dehumanization, this specific act is a persistent falsehood.

Source URL: https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/shareena-clanton-is-like-many-of-us-a-victim-of-the-flora-and-fauna-act-myth/m40dgtgah

## Final Judgement
Based on the recorded evidence, the key insight is that no Australian law titled "The Flora and Fauna Act" ever existed. Multiple sources, including historians and fact-checkers, have thoroughly debunked this claim, identifying it as a pervasive myth that likely originated from a symbolic description of discriminatory treatment in the 1970s, not from any actual legislation. While Aboriginal Australians faced severe legal discrimination and were administered by bodies like the "Aborigines and Fisheries" department, there is no evidence of a specific act classifying them as animals or flora.

The information is conclusive; no further investigation is required to finalize the fact-check.

The evidence clearly and explicitly refutes the core claim that a law called "The Flora and Fauna Act" classified Aboriginal people as animals until it was overturned in the 1960s. Therefore, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is **refuted** because no law titled "The Flora and Fauna Act" ever existed in Australia, making it a [well-documented urban myth](https://www.sbs.com.au/nitv/article/shareena-clanton-is-like-many-of-us-a-victim-of-the-flora-and-fauna-act-myth/m40dgtgah). While Aboriginal Australians faced profound legal discrimination and were administered by departments with names like "Aborigines and Fisheries," there is no evidence of legislation that explicitly classified them as animals or flora ([ABC News](https://www.abc.net.au/news/2018-03-20/fact-check-flora-and-fauna-1967-referendum/9550650)). The 1967 referendum did alter the Constitution to allow the federal government to make laws for Aboriginal people, but it did not overturn any non-existent fauna-related act ([ABC News](https://www.abc.net.au/news/2017-05-26/larissa-behrendt-mythbusting-the-1967-referendum/8349858)).